course: biology
lesson: 28
title: Sharks
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A shark is a fish with no bones at all: its skeleton is cartilage, the bendy stuff in your nose and ears, so it is light and flexible, and because cartilage rots away, shark fossils are mostly teeth.
explain: Those teeth sit in rows like a conveyor belt; when one breaks a new one moves forward, so some sharks lose tens of thousands in a lifetime, and that is why a sandy beach can be full of tiny black shark teeth.
explain: Sharks have senses you don't: pores on the snout, the ampullae of Lorenzini, feel the faint electricity of a fish's muscles, so a shark can find a fish hiding under the sand.
explain: Films like Jaws show sharks hunting people; in truth, sharks kill only a handful of people a year, usually by mistaking a swimmer for a seal, while people kill around 100 million sharks a year.
explain: And the biggest shark of all, the whale shark, up to 12 to 18 metres long, eats only plankton and tiny fish and lets snorkellers swim beside it.
fact: Sharks lose teeth all the time and grow new ones from the rows behind, some tens of thousands in a lifetime.
fact: A shark's skeleton is made of cartilage, which is lighter and bendier than bone, so shark fossils are mostly teeth.
fact: Great white sharks often attack seals from below and can burst right out of the water; they grow to about 6 metres and can live over 70 years.
fact: Sharks smell blood well, but the story that they can smell one drop from miles away is exaggerated.
fact: Sharks don't hunt humans; bites are very rare and often a mistake, and people kill around 100 million sharks a year.
fact: The whale shark is the biggest fish in the world, up to about 12 to 18 metres, filters plankton and is harmless to people.
fact: Some sharks, like great whites and makos, must keep swimming to push water over their gills, while nurse sharks can rest on the bottom.
fact: Sharks have no swim bladder, so many sink if they stop swimming.
fact: Sharks sense the faint electricity of living animals with pores on their snouts called the ampullae of Lorenzini.
fact: Shark skin is covered in tiny tooth-like scales called dermal denticles that help water flow smoothly, and some swimsuits copy them.
fact: Hammerheads have eyes at the ends of a wide flat head, which gives better all-round vision and spreads out their electric sensors.
fact: The Greenland shark is the longest-living animal with a backbone known, probably at least 270 years, growing about a centimetre a year.
fact: Some sharks and rays lay eggs in leathery cases called mermaid's purses, while other sharks give birth to live pups.
fact: Many sharks are endangered because they grow slowly and have few young, and they keep oceans healthy by eating sick fish.
exercise: Why is a beach sometimes full of tiny black shark teeth? | Sharks lose and replace teeth all their lives | Sharks spit out their old teeth on purpose to mark their own beach | The teeth are from one huge shark that died | Sharks lose all their teeth once a year
exercise: What is a shark's skeleton made of? | Cartilage | Hard bone, like ours | Shell, like a crab | Bits of coral
exercise: Why are shark fossils mostly teeth? | Cartilage rots away but teeth last | Sharks have no other body parts that could turn to stone | People take the rest home | Shark bones melt in salt water
exercise: How does a great white often catch a seal? | It rushes up from below and may burst out of the water | It chases it slowly across the beach | It traps it in a net of bubbles | It waits on the ice for the seal to fall asleep, then grabs it from above
exercise: Why do most shark bites on people happen? | The shark mistakes a swimmer for a seal or fish | Sharks hunt people as their main food | Sharks are angry at swimmers | Sharks always attack anything in the water that wears the colour red
exercise: About how many sharks do people kill each year? | Around 100 million | Around 100 | Around 10,000 in the whole world | Around 10 billion
exercise: What does a whale shark eat? | Plankton and tiny fish | Seals and penguins | Other whale sharks and their eggs | Squid and octopus
exercise: Why must a great white keep swimming? | To push water over its gills to breathe | To stop its skin drying out | Because it has no fins to float with | To keep its teeth clean
exercise: Why do many sharks sink if they stop swimming? | They have no swim bladder | Their teeth are far too heavy | Their skin soaks up the water | They hold their breath too long
exercise: How can a shark find a fish hidden under the sand? | It senses the faint electricity of the fish's muscles | It sees through the sand | It hears the fish's heartbeat | It tastes the fish from far away with its tongue and lips
exercise: Why does a shark's skin feel like sandpaper the wrong way? | It is covered in tiny tooth-like scales | It is covered all over in tiny grains of sand that stick to it | It has hairs like a cat | It is made of cartilage
exercise: Why do hammerheads have such wide heads? | For better all-round vision and spread-out electric sensors | To hammer open shellfish | To carry their babies | To steer like a boat's rudder and nothing more, as their tails are weak
exercise: About how long can a Greenland shark live? | At least about 270 years | About 7 years | About 27 years | About 2,700 years, older than the pyramids
exercise: What is a mermaid's purse? | A leathery egg case of a shark or ray | A shell where mermaids keep money | A jellyfish washed up on the beach | A seaweed pod full of seeds that wash up on the beach
exercise: Why are many sharks endangered? | Overfishing, and they grow slowly with few young | They eat far too much and then starve when the fish run out | The sea is too cold for them | They fight each other to death
exercise: How do sharks help keep oceans healthy? | They eat sick fish and keep other animals in balance | They clean the seabed of sand | They make oxygen with their fins | They don't, the sea would be better and safer without any of them
test: the teacher says sharks got no bones. is she having a laugh? | No, she's right, a shark's skeleton is cartilage | Yes, sharks have more bones than us | Yes, only baby sharks lack bones | No, sharks have no skeleton at all
test: In Jaws the shark hunts people on purpose. What's closer to the truth? | Bites are very rare and often a case of mistaken identity | Sharks hunt people every day | Sharks only ever eat people who can't swim well and splash about a lot | Sharks hunt boats, not people
test: Count them: a shark has a row of five gill slits on each side. How many gill slits is that? | 10 | 5 | 15 | 20
test: What would happen to a great white if it got stuck in a net and couldn't swim? | It could not get enough water over its gills to breathe | It would rest safely on the bottom like a nurse shark does all day | It would float to the top and sunbathe | It would breathe air instead
test: My uncle says a shark can smell one drop of blood from miles away. Is that right? | No, that is exaggerated; sharks smell blood well, but not from miles off | Yes, any shark can smell blood across a whole ocean | Yes, but only great whites | No, sharks cannot smell at all
test: Why would a swimsuit maker copy shark skin? | Its tiny scales help water flow smoothly, so you swim faster | It makes the swimmer look big and scary to the sharks in the water | It keeps the swimmer warm | It stops sharks noticing you
test: dude a whale shark is swimming right next to me, am i gonna get eaten?? | No, it eats only plankton and tiny fish | Yes, it eats anything that big | Yes, but only if you splash about and kick your legs | No, because it has no mouth
test: If many sharks were killed in one sea, what might happen to the other fish? | Sick fish would spread disease and the balance would be upset | All the other fish would get much healthier at once and live far longer | The fish would turn into sharks | Nothing, sharks don't eat fish
test: A Greenland shark grows about a centimetre a year and is 4 metres long. What does that tell you? | It must be very, very old | It must be about 4 years old | It must have grown fast as a baby | It must be a young whale shark
test: In Finding Nemo the shark promises "fish are friends, not food". What do real sharks eat? | Many eat fish, seals or squid | Real sharks eat only green seaweed | Every shark eats only plankton | Real sharks eat only swimmers and surfers
test: Why do you think sharks' numbers fall so fast when many are caught? | They grow slowly and have few young | They lay millions of eggs | They live only a year anyway | They swim into nets on purpose
exam: Identify the material of which the skeleton of a shark is principally composed. | Cartilage | Ossified bone | Calcium shell | Calcified coral
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the threat posed by sharks to human beings? | Fatal incidents are exceedingly rare and frequently attributable to mistaken identity | Sharks habitually pursue human beings as their principal and preferred prey in every ocean | Sharks attack all persons who enter the water | The threat exceeds that which humans pose to sharks
exam: Ascertain the faculty by which a shark may detect prey concealed beneath the sand. | Electroreception by means of the ampullae of Lorenzini | Vision through the sand | Detection of light emitted by the prey | Taste at a great distance
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the longevity of the Greenland shark. | It is believed to attain at least two hundred and seventy years | It attains no more than seven years | It attains approximately twenty-seven years | It is immortal
